  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cup B$ is represented by the area that is inside both circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242110/assets/ad51768e560488687b0dfbd573f71136f7863106d0a830828878bc5488b05daf.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A\\cup B$ is\n\n$$\n6+1+4=11.\n$$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n6+1+4+12=23.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=\\frac{11}{23}≈0.48\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
